<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-types-of-relations" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Types of Relations</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.0 Calculus
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>
                        4.1 Functions
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title> 
                    <p>
                        Identify relations and the different types of mappings
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>
                        11
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>
                        40 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on activity -->

        <axiom component="lesson-plan">
            <title>Guidance on the Opening Activity</title>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Suggested Time</title>
                    <p>
                        10 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Objective</title>
                    <p>
                        To help learners build intuition about how inputs and outputs can be connected before any formal definitions are introduced. The activity moves from familiar everyday relationships to pairs of numbers, so learners are not working in the abstract from the start.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                    <p>
                        Facilitate a short paired discussion. Listen for learners who notice that some relationships allow one person to connect to many others, while other relationships force a strict one-to-one pairing. Avoid confirming or correcting at this stage, the goal is for learners to surface their own observations that you will formalise in the instruction that follows.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
        </axiom>

    <!-- Add Activity -->

    <p>
        <term> A relation</term> establishes a connection between elements of a starting set (the inputs) and elements of a destination set (the outputs). In mathematics, the behavior of a relation depends entirely on how these inputs and outputs are mapped to one another.
    </p>
    <p>
        Some relations assign exactly one unique output to each input, while others might map a single input to multiple outputs, or multiple inputs to the same output. Recognizing these mapping patterns is a crucial step in determining whether a specific relation qualifies as a function.
    </p>

    <activity xml:id="activity-exploring-relations">
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Before looking at pairs of numbers, think about everyday relationships between people. Consider the following real-world scenarios and discuss them with a partner.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Think about the relationship between a child and their father. Can one child have more than one biological father? Can one father have more than one child?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Think about the relationship between a student and the school clubs they belong to. Can one student join more than one club? Can one club have more than one student?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Think about the relationship between a daughter and her mother. Can one daughter have more than one biological mother? Can one mother have more than one daughter?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Think about the relationship between a brother and his siblings. Can one brother have more than one sibling? Can the same sibling be connected to more than one brother in the family?
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    For each scenario above, describe in your own words how the inputs (the first person or group) connect to the outputs (the second person or group). Try to group the scenarios based on similarities in how the connections work.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Now look at the diagrams below. Each diagram shows how items on the left connect to items on the right. Describe how the arrows are arranged and decide which diagrams show functions.
                </p>

                <sidebyside widths="48% 48%">
                    <figure xml:id="fig-map-one-to-one-activity">
                        <caption>Each item points to one different item</caption>
                        <image width="100%" xml:id="img-map-one-to-one-activity">
                            <latex-image>
                                \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth, scale=0.8]
                                    \node at (0,4) {Left group};
                                    \node at (5,4) {Right group};
                                    \foreach \y/\a/\b in {3/1/2,2/2/4,1/3/6}
                                    {
                                        \node[circle,draw,minimum size=7mm] (L\a) at (0,\y) {$\a$};
                                        \node[circle,draw,minimum size=7mm] (R\b) at (5,\y) {$\b$};
                                        \draw[-&gt;,thick] (L\a) -- (R\b);
                                    }
                                \end{tikzpicture}
                            </latex-image>
                        </image>
                    </figure>

                    <figure xml:id="fig-map-many-to-one-activity">
                        <caption>Many items point to the same item</caption>
                        <image width="100%" xml:id="img-map-many-to-one-activity">
                            <latex-image>
                                \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth, scale=0.8]
                                    \node at (0,4) {Left group};
                                    \node at (5,4) {Right group};
                                    \foreach \y/\a in {3/1,2/2,1/3}
                                    {
                                        \node[circle,draw,minimum size=7mm] (L\a) at (0,\y) {$\a$};
                                    }
                                    \node[circle,draw,minimum size=7mm] (R5) at (5,2) {$5$};
                                    \foreach \a in {1,2,3}
                                    {
                                        \draw[-&gt;,thick] (L\a) -- (R5);
                                    }
                                \end{tikzpicture}
                            </latex-image>
                        </image>
                    </figure>
                </sidebyside>

                <sidebyside widths="48% 48%">
                    <figure xml:id="fig-map-one-to-many-activity">
                        <caption>One item points to many items</caption>
                        <image width="100%" xml:id="img-map-one-to-many-activity">
                            <latex-image>
                                \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth, scale=0.8]
                                    \node at (0,4) {Left group};
                                    \node at (5,4) {Right group};
                                    \node[circle,draw,minimum size=7mm] (L1) at (0,2) {$1$};
                                    \foreach \y/\b in {3/2,2/4,1/6}
                                    {
                                        \node[circle,draw,minimum size=7mm] (R\b) at (5,\y) {$\b$};
                                        \draw[-&gt;,thick] (L1) -- (R\b);
                                    }
                                \end{tikzpicture}
                            </latex-image>
                        </image>
                    </figure>

                    <figure xml:id="fig-map-many-to-many-activity">
                        <caption>Many items point to many items</caption>
                        <image width="100%" xml:id="img-map-many-to-many-activity">
                            <latex-image>
                                \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth, scale=0.8]
                                    \node at (0,4) {Left group};
                                    \node at (5,4) {Right group};
                                    \node[circle,draw,minimum size=7mm] (L1) at (0,2.7) {$1$};
                                    \node[circle,draw,minimum size=7mm] (L2) at (0,1.3) {$2$};
                                    \node[circle,draw,minimum size=7mm] (R2) at (5,3) {$2$};
                                    \node[circle,draw,minimum size=7mm] (R3) at (5,2) {$3$};
                                    \node[circle,draw,minimum size=7mm] (R4) at (5,1) {$4$};
                                    \draw[-&gt;,thick] (L1) -- (R2);
                                    \draw[-&gt;,thick] (L1) -- (R3);
                                    \draw[-&gt;,thick] (L2) -- (R3);
                                    \draw[-&gt;,thick] (L2) -- (R4);
                                \end{tikzpicture}
                            </latex-image>
                        </image>
                    </figure>
                </sidebyside>
            </statement>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <!-- [LP] Add guidance on structured lesson plan-->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Suggested Time</title>
                    <p>
                        15 minutes
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Objective</title>
                    <p>
                        To formalise the concepts and properties learners discovered in the opening activity by giving precise names and definitions to each mapping type.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Key Takeaways and Teacher Connection</title>
            <p>
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Connecting to Learner Work</alert> — Draw an explicit link between the real-world scenarios in the activity and the formal terms. For example, the child-to-father relationship (one child, one father) connects directly to the idea of a one-to-one mapping, while the mother-to-children relationship (one mother, many children) connects to one-to-many. Naming the types using the learners' own examples will help the definitions feel grounded rather than abstract.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Function or Not</alert> — Emphasise that the key question is always whether each input maps to exactly one output. One-to-one and many-to-one mappings both satisfy this condition, which is why they are functions. One-to-many and many-to-many mappings do not, because at least one input connects to more than one output.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <alert>Common Confusion</alert> — Learners often accept many-to-one as a function without difficulty but struggle to explain why one-to-many fails. Spend extra time on this distinction, and return to the library borrower example (one borrower checking out multiple books) to make it concrete before moving to the formal definition.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </axiom>

    <!-- Add insight / key takeaway directly related to activity / learner experience -->

    <insight>
        <paragraphs xml:id="para-relation-concepts">
            <title>Types of Relations</title>

            <p>
                The way inputs are connected to outputs determines the type of relation. Some relations connect each input to one output, while others connect one input to several outputs or allow different inputs to share the same output. The table below summarises the four main types of relations and shows whether each one is a function.
            </p>

            <table xml:id="table-relation-types">
                <title>Summary of Relation Types</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                        <cell>Type</cell>
                        <cell>Description</cell>
                        <cell>Example</cell>
                        <cell>Function</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell><term>One-to-One</term></cell>
                        <cell>Each input is connected to one different output.</cell>
                        <cell>Each player wears one jersey number.</cell>
                        <cell>Yes</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell><term>Many-to-One</term></cell>
                        <cell>Different inputs are connected to the same output.</cell>
                        <cell>Several rivers flow into the same lake.</cell>
                        <cell>Yes</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell><term>One-to-Many</term></cell>
                        <cell>One input is connected to more than one output.</cell>
                        <cell>One library member borrows several books.</cell>
                        <cell>No</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell><term>Many-to-Many</term></cell>
                        <cell>Several inputs are connected to several outputs.</cell>
                        <cell>Farmers grow different crops and each crop is grown by many farmers.</cell>
                        <cell>No</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <p>
                A relation is a function when every input has only one output. This means that one-to-one and many-to-one relations are functions. One-to-many and many-to-many relations are <alert>not</alert> functions because at least one input is connected to more than one output.
            </p>

            <p>
                Use the interactive below to build your own relations. Click an object in Set <m>A</m>, then an object in Set <m>B</m>, to draw an arrow between them; click an arrow to remove it. As you connect the objects, the activity names the type of relation and tells you whether it is a function. Try to make each of the four types, and notice what happens to a Set <m>A</m> object as soon as it points to more than one output.
            </p>

            <interactive xml:id="interactive-relation-types" platform="jsxgraph" width="90%" aspect="1:1" source="jsxgraph/relation-types.js">
                <sbsgroup>
                    <sidebyside width="95%" margins="2%">
                        <slate xml:id="jsx-relation-types" surface="jsxboard" aspect="1:1"/>
                    </sidebyside>
                    <sidebyside width="95%" margins="2%">
                        <slate surface="html">
                            <div style="text-align:center;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
                                <input type="button" value="Clear" onclick="clearRelation();" onkeypress="clearRelation();" style="margin:3px;font-size:14px;padding:3px 10px;"/>
                            </div>
                        </slate>
                    </sidebyside>
                </sbsgroup>
            </interactive>

        </paragraphs>
    </insight>

    <paragraphs xml:id="para-line-tests-concepts">
        <title>The Line Tests</title>
        <p>
            When a relation is graphed on a coordinate plane, we can quickly determine its properties using visual tests.
        </p>
        <definition xml:id="def-vertical-line-test">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The <term>Vertical Line Test</term> states that a graph represents a function if and only if no vertical line intersects the graph at more than one point. If any vertical line cuts the graph multiple times, it means one input (<m>x</m>-value) maps to multiple outputs (<m>y</m>-values).
                </p>
                <sidebyside widths="45% 45%" margins="0% 10%">
                    <figure xml:id="fig-vlt-pass">
                        <caption>Passes the Vertical Line Test — this is a function.</caption>
                        <image xml:id="img-vlt-pass">
                            <latex-image>
                                \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth, scale=0.75]
                                    \draw[help lines, color=gray!30, dashed] (-2.5,-2.5) grid (2.5,2.5);
                                    \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (-2.5,0) -- (2.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                    \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (0,-2.5) -- (0,2.5) node[above] {$y$};
                                    \draw[ultra thick, blue, domain=-1.5:1.5, samples=50] plot (\x, {\x*\x});
                                    \draw[thick, red, dashed, &lt;-&gt;] (0.8, -2.5) -- (0.8, 2.5);
                                    \filldraw[red] (0.8, 0.64) circle (3pt);
                                    \node[red, right] at (0.8, 0.64) {\small 1 point};
                                \end{tikzpicture}
                            </latex-image>
                        </image>
                    </figure>
                    <figure xml:id="fig-vlt-fail">
                        <caption>Fails the Vertical Line Test — this is not a function.</caption>
                        <image xml:id="img-vlt-fail">
                            <latex-image>
                                \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth, scale=0.75]
                                    \draw[help lines, color=gray!30, dashed] (-2.5,-2.5) grid (2.5,2.5);
                                    \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (-2.5,0) -- (2.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                    \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (0,-2.5) -- (0,2.5) node[above] {$y$};
                                    \draw[ultra thick, blue] (0,0) circle (2cm);
                                    \draw[thick, red, dashed, &lt;-&gt;] (1, -2.5) -- (1, 2.5);
                                    \filldraw[red] (1, 1.732) circle (3pt);
                                    \filldraw[red] (1, -1.732) circle (3pt);
                                    \node[red, right] at (1, 1.732) {\small 2 points};
                                \end{tikzpicture}
                            </latex-image>
                        </image>
                    </figure>
                </sidebyside>
            </statement>
        </definition>
        <definition xml:id="def-horizontal-line-test">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The <term>Horizontal Line Test</term> is used on a known function to check for uniqueness. A function is one-to-one if and only if no horizontal line intersects its graph at more than one point. If a horizontal line crosses multiple times, it means different inputs share the same output, making it many-to-one.
                </p>
                <sidebyside widths="45% 45%" margins="0% 10%">
                    <figure xml:id="fig-hlt-pass">
                        <caption>Passes the Horizontal Line Test — this function is one-to-one.</caption>
                        <image xml:id="img-hlt-pass">
                            <latex-image>
                                \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth, scale=0.75]
                                    \draw[help lines, color=gray!30, dashed] (-2.5,-2.5) grid (2.5,2.5);
                                    \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (-2.5,0) -- (2.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                    \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (0,-2.5) -- (0,2.5) node[above] {$y$};
                                    \draw[ultra thick, blue, domain=-1.4:1.4, samples=50] plot (\x, {\x*\x*\x});
                                    \draw[thick, red, dashed, &lt;-&gt;] (-2.5, 0.8) -- (2.5, 0.8);
                                    \filldraw[red] (0.928, 0.8) circle (3pt);
                                    \node[red, above right] at (0.928, 0.8) {\small 1 point};
                                \end{tikzpicture}
                            </latex-image>
                        </image>
                    </figure>
                    <figure xml:id="fig-hlt-fail">
                        <caption>Fails the Horizontal Line Test — this function is many-to-one.</caption>
                        <image xml:id="img-hlt-fail">
                            <latex-image>
                                \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth, scale=0.75]
                                    \draw[help lines, color=gray!30, dashed] (-2.5,-0.5) grid (2.5,2.5);
                                    \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (-2.5,0) -- (2.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
                                    \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (0,-0.5) -- (0,2.5) node[above] {$y$};
                                    \draw[ultra thick, blue, domain=-1.55:1.55, samples=50] plot (\x, {\x*\x});
                                    \draw[thick, red, dashed, &lt;-&gt;] (-2.5, 1.5) -- (2.5, 1.5);
                                    \filldraw[red] (-1.225, 1.5) circle (3pt);
                                    \filldraw[red] (1.225, 1.5) circle (3pt);
                                    \node[red, above] at (0, 1.5) {\small 2 points};
                                \end{tikzpicture}
                            </latex-image>
                        </image>
                    </figure>
                </sidebyside>
            </statement>
        </definition>
    </paragraphs>
    <!-- Add further instruction (not in a block) to develop ideas as needed -->

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- Add at least 3 examples -->
    <!-- Replace ex-identifying-relations with this -->

    <example xml:id="ex-identifying-relations">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Examine each mapping diagram below. Identify the type of relation shown, and determine whether the relation is a function.
            </p>

            <sidebyside widths="48% 48%">
                <figure xml:id="fig-ex-one-to-one">
                    <caption>Diagram A</caption>
                    <image width="100%" xml:id="img-ex-one-to-one">
                        <latex-image>
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth, scale=0.8]
                                \node at (0,4) {Left group};
                                \node at (5,4) {Right group};
                                \foreach \y/\a/\b in {3/1/2,2/2/4,1/3/6}
                                {
                                    \node[circle,draw,minimum size=7mm] (L\a) at (0,\y) {$\a$};
                                    \node[circle,draw,minimum size=7mm] (R\b) at (5,\y) {$\b$};
                                    \draw[-&gt;,thick] (L\a) -- (R\b);
                                }
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>

                <figure xml:id="fig-ex-many-to-one">
                    <caption>Diagram B</caption>
                    <image width="100%" xml:id="img-ex-many-to-one">
                        <latex-image>
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth, scale=0.8]
                                \node at (0,4) {Left group};
                                \node at (5,4) {Right group};
                                \foreach \y/\a in {3/1,2/2,1/3}
                                {
                                    \node[circle,draw,minimum size=7mm] (L\a) at (0,\y) {$\a$};
                                }
                                \node[circle,draw,minimum size=7mm] (R5) at (5,2) {$5$};
                                \foreach \a in {1,2,3}
                                {
                                    \draw[-&gt;,thick] (L\a) -- (R5);
                                }
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </sidebyside>

            <sidebyside widths="48% 48%">
                <figure xml:id="fig-ex-one-to-many">
                    <caption>Diagram C</caption>
                    <image width="100%" xml:id="img-ex-one-to-many">
                        <latex-image>
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth, scale=0.8]
                                \node at (0,4) {Left group};
                                \node at (5,4) {Right group};
                                \node[circle,draw,minimum size=7mm] (L2) at (0,2) {$2$};
                                \foreach \y/\b in {3/1,2/3,1/5}
                                {
                                    \node[circle,draw,minimum size=7mm] (R\b) at (5,\y) {$\b$};
                                    \draw[-&gt;,thick] (L2) -- (R\b);
                                }
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>

                <figure xml:id="fig-ex-many-to-many">
                    <caption>Diagram D</caption>
                    <image width="100%" xml:id="img-ex-many-to-many">
                        <latex-image>
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth, scale=0.8]
                                \node[circle,draw,minimum size=7mm] (L1) at (0,2.7) {$1$};
                                \node[circle,draw,minimum size=7mm] (L2) at (0,1.3) {$2$};
                                \node[circle,draw,minimum size=7mm] (R2) at (5,3) {$2$};
                                \node[circle,draw,minimum size=7mm] (R3) at (5,2) {$3$};
                                \node[circle,draw,minimum size=7mm] (R4) at (5,1) {$4$};
                                \draw[-&gt;,thick] (L1) -- (R2);
                                \draw[-&gt;,thick] (L1) -- (R3);
                                \draw[-&gt;,thick] (L2) -- (R3);
                                \draw[-&gt;,thick] (L2) -- (R4);
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </sidebyside>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Diagram A shows a one-to-one relation: each element on the left maps to exactly one distinct element on the right. This satisfies the definition of a function.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Diagram B shows a many-to-one relation: several elements on the left map to the same single element on the right. Since every left-hand element still has exactly one output, this is a function.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Diagram C shows a one-to-many relation: a single element on the left maps to multiple elements on the right. Because this element has more than one output, this is not a function.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Diagram D shows a many-to-many relation: multiple elements on the left each map to multiple elements on the right. Since at least one left-hand element has more than one output, this is not a function.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-vertical-line-test">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Determine whether the graph of the function <m>f(x)=\dfrac{1}{x-2}</m> represents a function.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-vlt-rational">
                <caption>Applying the Vertical Line Test to <m>f(x)=\dfrac{1}{x-2}</m>.</caption>
                <image width="50%" xml:id="img-vlt-rational">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth, scale=0.75]
                            \draw[help lines, color=gray!30, dashed] (-6,-4) grid (7,4);

                            \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (-6.5,0) -- (7.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
                            \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (0,-4.5) -- (0,4.5) node[above] {$y$};

                            % Asymptotes
                            \draw[dashed, gray] (2,-4.5) -- (2,4.5);
                            \draw[dashed, gray] (-6.5,0) -- (7.5,0);

                            % Function
                            \draw[ultra thick, blue, domain=-6:1.85, samples=200]
                                plot (\x,{1/(\x-2)});
                            \draw[ultra thick, blue, domain=2.15:7, samples=200]
                                plot (\x,{1/(\x-2)});
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                Draw a vertical line such as <m>x=1</m>. As shown in <xref ref="fig-vlt-rational-soln"/>, the line intersects the graph at only one point. In fact, every vertical line meets the graph at most once. Therefore, the graph passes the Vertical Line Test and represents a function.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-vlt-rational-soln">
                <caption>Applying the Vertical Line Test to <m>f(x)=\dfrac{1}{x-2}</m>.</caption>
                <image width="50%" xml:id="img-vlt-rational-soln">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth, scale=0.75]
                            \draw[help lines, color=gray!30, dashed] (-6,-4) grid (7,4);

                            \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (-6.5,0) -- (7.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
                            \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (0,-4.5) -- (0,4.5) node[above] {$y$};

                            % Asymptotes
                            \draw[dashed, gray] (2,-4.5) -- (2,4.5);
                            \draw[dashed, gray] (-6.5,0) -- (7.5,0);

                            % Function
                            \draw[ultra thick, blue, domain=-6:1.85, samples=200]
                                plot (\x,{1/(\x-2)});
                            \draw[ultra thick, blue, domain=2.15:7, samples=200]
                                plot (\x,{1/(\x-2)});

                            % Vertical line
                            \draw[red, thick, dashed, &lt;-&gt;] (1,-4.5) -- (1,4.5)
                                node[above right] {$x=1$};

                            % Intersection
                            \filldraw[red] (1,-1) circle (2.5pt);
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </solution>
    </example>


    <example xml:id="ex-horizontal-line-test">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Determine whether the function <m>f(x)=|x|</m> is one-to-one.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-hlt-absolute-value">
                <caption>Applying the Horizontal Line Test to <m>f(x)=|x|</m>.</caption>
                <image width="50%" xml:id="img-hlt-absolute-value">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth]
                            \draw[help lines, color=gray!30, dashed] (-3,-1) grid (3,3);
                            \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (-3,0) -- (3,0) node[right] {$x$};
                            \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (0,-1) -- (0,3) node[above] {$y$};
                            \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-2.5,2.5) -- (0,0) -- (2.5,2.5);
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                Draw a horizontal line such as <m>y=2</m>. As shown in <xref ref="fig-hlt-absolute-value-soln"/>, the line meets the graph at the points <m>(-2,2)</m> and <m>(2,2)</m>. Since one horizontal line cuts the graph at two points, the function fails the Horizontal Line Test. Therefore, <m>f(x)=|x|</m> is <alert>not</alert> one-to-one.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-hlt-absolute-value-soln">
                <caption>Applying the Horizontal Line Test to <m>f(x)=|x|</m>.</caption>
                <image width="50%" xml:id="img-hlt-absolute-value-soln">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth]
                            \draw[help lines, color=gray!30, dashed] (-3,-1) grid (3,3);
                            \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (-3,0) -- (3,0) node[right] {$x$};
                            \draw[&lt;-&gt;, thick] (0,-1) -- (0,3) node[above] {$y$};
                            \draw[ultra thick, blue] (-2.5,2.5) -- (0,0) -- (2.5,2.5);
                            \draw[thick, red, dashed, &lt;-&gt;] (-3,2) -- (3,2) node[below left] {$y=2$};
                            \filldraw[red] (-2,2) circle (3pt);
                            \filldraw[red] (2,2) circle (3pt);
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <!-- If STACK questions are included, uncomment this: -->

    <!-- <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/checkpoint.ptx" /> -->

    <!-- Add STACK exercises if made -->

    <!-- Add static exercises -->

    <!-- Replace exercises-types-of-relations with this -->



<exercises xml:id="exercises-types-of-relations">

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The diagram below shows how four football players were assigned jersey numbers before a match. Identify the type of relation and state whether it is a function.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-exercise-player-jersey">
                <caption>Players and jersey numbers</caption>
                <image width="55%" xml:id="img-exercise-player-jersey">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth,scale=0.85]
                            \node at (0,4){Players};
                            \node at (5,4){Jersey numbers};

                            \foreach \y/\a/\b in {3/Ali/7,2/Brian/10,1/Chris/15}
                            {
                                \node[draw,rounded corners] (L\a) at (0,\y){\a};
                                \node[circle,draw] (R\b) at (5,\y){\b};
                                \draw[-&gt;,thick] (L\a)--(R\b);
                            }
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                This is a one-to-one relation. It is a function because every player has exactly one jersey number.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A farmer labels one sack of maize for every harvest season. Several sacks may come from the same farm, but each sack belongs to only one season. Identify the type of relation between sacks and seasons. Is it a function
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                This is a many-to-one relation. It is a function because each sack is linked to only one harvest season.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A traveller buys one bus ticket that allows travel on three different buses during the day. Identify the type of relation between the ticket and the buses. Is it a function
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                This is a one-to-many relation. It is not a function because one ticket is linked to several buses.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The map below shows rivers flowing into lakes. Some rivers flow into one lake while another river branches into two lakes. Examine the diagram and decide whether it represents a function.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-exercise-rivers">
                <caption>Rivers and lakes</caption>
                <image width="55%" xml:id="img-exercise-rivers">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth,scale=0.85]
                            \node at (0,4){Rivers};
                            \node at (5,4){Lakes};

                            \node[draw,rounded corners] (R1) at (0,3){River A};
                            \node[draw,rounded corners] (R2) at (0,1.5){River B};

                            \node[draw,ellipse] (L1) at (5,3){Lake X};
                            \node[draw,ellipse] (L2) at (5,1.5){Lake Y};

                            \draw[-&gt;,thick](R1)--(L1);
                            \draw[-&gt;,thick](R2)--(L1);
                            \draw[-&gt;,thick](R2)--(L2);
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                It is not a function because River B is linked to two lakes.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The graph below shows the relation <m>y^2=x</m>. Decide whether it represents a function.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-exercise-sideways-parabola-vlt-quiz">
                <caption>Testing the graph with a vertical line</caption>
                <image width="55%" xml:id="img-exercise-sideways-parabola-vlt-quiz">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth,scale=0.75]
                            \draw[help lines,color=gray!30,dashed] (-1,-3) grid (5,3);
                            \draw[&lt;-&gt;] (-1,0)--(5,0) node[right]{$x$};
                            \draw[&lt;-&gt;] (0,-3)--(0,3) node[above]{$y$};

                            \draw[ultra thick,blue,domain=-2:2,samples=60,variable=\t]
                                plot({\t*\t},{\t});
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                It is not a function because one vertical line meets the graph at two points.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-exercise-sideways-parabola-vlt-answer">
                <caption>Testing the graph with a vertical line</caption>
                <image width="55%" xml:id="img-exercise-sideways-parabola-vlt-answer">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth,scale=0.75]
                            \draw[help lines,color=gray!30,dashed] (-1,-3) grid (5,3);
                            \draw[&lt;-&gt;] (-1,0)--(5,0) node[right]{$x$};
                            \draw[&lt;-&gt;] (0,-3)--(0,3) node[above]{$y$};

                            \draw[ultra thick,blue,domain=-2:2,samples=60,variable=\t]
                                plot({\t*\t},{\t});

                            \draw[red,dashed,&lt;-&gt;](2.5,-3)--(2.5,3);

                            \filldraw[red](2.5,1.58)circle(3pt);
                            \filldraw[red](2.5,-1.58)circle(3pt);
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </answer>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The graph below shows <m>f(x)=x^3</m>. Use the horizontal line to decide whether the function is one-to-one.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-exercise-cubic-hlt-quiz">
                <caption>Testing the graph with a horizontal line</caption>
                <image width="55%" xml:id="img-exercise-cubic-hlt-quiz">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth,scale=0.75]
                            \draw[help lines,color=gray!30,dashed] (-3,-3) grid (3,3);
                            \draw[&lt;-&gt;] (-3,0)--(3,0) node[right]{$x$};
                            \draw[&lt;-&gt;] (0,-3)--(0,3) node[above]{$y$};

                            \draw[ultra thick,blue,domain=-1.45:1.45,samples=80]
                                plot(\x,{\x*\x*\x});
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                Yes. Every horizontal line meets the graph at only one point, so the function is one-to-one.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-exercise-cubic-hlt-answer">
                <caption>Testing the graph with a horizontal line</caption>
                <image width="55%" xml:id="img-exercise-cubic-hlt-answer">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[&gt;=stealth,scale=0.75]
                            \draw[help lines,color=gray!30,dashed] (-3,-3) grid (3,3);
                            \draw[&lt;-&gt;] (-3,0)--(3,0) node[right]{$x$};
                            \draw[&lt;-&gt;] (0,-3)--(0,3) node[above]{$y$};

                            \draw[ultra thick,blue,domain=-1.45:1.45,samples=80]
                                plot(\x,{\x*\x*\x});

                            \draw[red,dashed,&lt;-&gt;](-3,1)--(3,1);

                            \filldraw[red](1,1)circle(3pt);
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </answer>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Every country has one capital city, but a capital city belongs to only one country. Identify the type of relation and state whether it is a function.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                This is a one-to-one relation. It is a function because each country is linked to one capital city.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A mobile phone number can receive messages from many different people. Identify the type of relation between senders and the phone number. Is it a function
            </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
            <p>
                This is a many-to-one relation. It is a function because each sender is linked to one phone number in this situation.
            </p>
        </answer>
    </exercise>

</exercises>
    <!-- [LP] Add information on how to differentiate  -->

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table>
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left">
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Learner Group</cell>
                    <cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Provide a reference card that shows a diagram of each mapping type alongside its real-world example (for instance, child-to-father for one-to-one and student-to-courses for many-to-many). Encourage these learners to check the diagram before deciding whether a mapping is a function, rather than trying to recall the definition from memory. Reduce the number of exercise questions and focus on parts (a) and (b) of each task before introducing the more complex mappings.
                    </cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        The core lesson activities as described above.
                    </cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell>
                    <cell>
                        Ask these learners to sketch their own graphs for each type of mapping and apply both the vertical and horizontal line tests to verify their classification. They can also be asked to come up with additional real-world examples of each mapping type that were not used in the lesson, and to explain in writing why their chosen example fits the category.
                    </cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>




       

    

